  "slug": "can-chiropractic-care-help-degenerative-disc-disease-new-research-on-neck-pain-and-spinal-disc-health",
  "title": "Can Chiropractic Care Help Degenerative Disc Disease? New Research on Neck Pain and Spinal Disc Health",
  "date": "2026-11-02",
  "excerpt": "Have you been diagnosed with degenerative disc disease, thinning spinal discs, or arthritis in your neck ? If so, you may be wondering whether anything can be done to…",
  "html": "<p>Have you been diagnosed with <strong>degenerative disc disease, thinning spinal discs, or arthritis in your neck</strong>? If so, you may be wondering whether anything can be done to improve your condition without surgery.</p>\n<p>The good news is that conservative treatment may help reduce pain, improve mobility, and restore function. A 2026 study published in <em>Bioengineering</em> adds to this discussion by examining the relationship between spinal alignment, cervical disc height, and neck pain following a specialized chiropractic rehabilitation program.</p>\n<p>At <strong>Advanced Back &amp; Neck Pain Center in Newark, Delaware</strong>, we believe understanding the spine is an important first step toward finding an appropriate treatment plan.</p>\n<h2>What Is Degenerative Disc Disease?</h2>\n<p>Your spinal discs sit between the bones of your spine, acting as cushions that help absorb forces and allow movement. Over time, these discs can lose hydration and height, develop structural changes, and become less flexible.</p>\n<p>These age-related changes are commonly called <strong>degenerative disc disease (DDD)</strong>. Despite the name, disc degeneration is not necessarily a disease in the traditional sense, and it doesn’t always cause pain.</p>\n<p>However, when symptoms occur, they may include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neck pain and stiffness</li>\n<li>Reduced spinal mobility</li>\n<li>Discomfort that extends into the shoulders</li>\n<li>Pain that interferes with daily activities</li>\n<li>In some cases, radiating arm pain or neurological symptoms</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Imaging findings alone don’t determine how much pain a person will experience. Some people have significant degenerative changes without symptoms, while others experience substantial discomfort with more modest findings.</p>\n<h2>What Does New Research Say About Chiropractic and Disc Health?</h2>\n<p>A 2026 study published in <em>Bioengineering</em> examined <strong>64 adults with neck pain, functional limitations, and degenerative changes in the cervical spine</strong>.</p>\n<p>The researchers investigated the results of a specialized rehabilitation approach known as Chiropractic BioPhysics® (CBP®), which combined specific chiropractic adjustments, therapeutic spinal exercises, and mechanical spinal traction.</p>\n<p>Patients completed an average of approximately 38 treatment visits over 19–20 weeks.</p>\n<p>Following treatment, the researchers reported several notable changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reduced neck pain:</strong> Average pain scores decreased from 6.66 out of 10 to 1.52.</li>\n<li><strong>Improved function:</strong> Average scores on the Neck Disability Index decreased from approximately 40% to 13%, indicating less self-reported disability.</li>\n<li><strong>Changes in spinal alignment:</strong> Measurements showed improvements in cervical curvature and forward-head posture.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased measured disc height:</strong> Average measurements of both the front and back portions of the cervical discs increased on follow-up X-rays.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These findings are encouraging because the patients experienced improvements in symptoms and function alongside measurable changes in spinal alignment and disc height.</p>\n<p>However, this study was a retrospective case series without a control group. As a result, researchers could not establish that the treatment alone caused the improvements or determine how much the results might differ from those of other treatments.</p>\n<p>The findings also do not establish that chiropractic care regenerates damaged disc tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Research reference:</strong> Katz EA, et al. <em>Increased Cervical Disc Height and Decreased Neck Pain and Disability Following Improvement in Cervical Lordosis and Posture Using Chiropractic BioPhysics.</em> Bioengineering. 2026;13(2):229.</p>\n<h2>How Can Chiropractic Care Help People With Disc Problems?</h2>\n<p>Chiropractic care may be one component of a conservative treatment plan for people experiencing neck pain and certain musculoskeletal problems associated with spinal degeneration.</p>\n<p>Depending on the patient’s examination findings and medical history, treatment may include:</p>\n<h3>1. Improving Spinal Mobility</h3>\n<p>Restricted spinal movement can make everyday activities uncomfortable. Appropriate manual therapy and chiropractic techniques may help improve mobility and reduce symptoms in selected patients.</p>\n<h3>2. Addressing Posture and Movement</h3>\n<p>Forward-head posture and other movement patterns may contribute to mechanical stress and discomfort in some people. A comprehensive assessment can help identify postural and movement factors worth addressing.</p>\n<h3>3. Incorporating Therapeutic Exercise</h3>\n<p>Exercises designed to improve strength, endurance, flexibility, and movement control can complement hands-on treatment. An individualized exercise program may also help patients maintain their functional improvements.</p>\n<h3>4. Supporting Everyday Function</h3>\n<p>The goal of conservative spine care is not simply to change an X-ray measurement. It is to help patients move more comfortably, participate in daily activities, and manage their symptoms.</p>\n<h2>Does a Degenerative Disc Diagnosis Mean You Need Surgery?</h2>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<p>Many people with degenerative disc disease can manage their symptoms with nonsurgical care. Depending on the diagnosis, this may include exercise, physical therapy, appropriate manual therapy, activity modification, and medical management.</p>\n<p>Surgery may be appropriate for certain conditions, particularly when there is significant nerve compression, progressive neurological impairment, or persistent disabling symptoms despite appropriate conservative treatment.</p>\n<p>The right approach depends on the individual’s symptoms, examination, imaging findings, and overall health.</p>\n<p>New research into chiropractic spinal rehabilitation is promising, but it should not be interpreted as evidence that every patient with disc degeneration can avoid surgery.</p>\n<h2>Looking for a Chiropractor in Newark, Delaware?</h2>\n<p>At <strong>Advanced Back &amp; Neck Pain Center</strong>, we take a comprehensive approach to back and neck pain. Our services include chiropractic care, physical therapy, and massage therapy, allowing us to consider different aspects of musculoskeletal health when developing an individualized treatment plan.</p>\n<p>If you’re experiencing neck pain, stiffness, or functional limitations associated with degenerative disc disease, our team can evaluate your condition and discuss whether conservative treatment may be appropriate.</p>\n<p>You don’t have to assume that disc degeneration automatically means surgery—or that nothing can be done to help.</p>\n<p><strong>Contact Advanced Back &amp; Neck Pain Center to learn more about your options for managing neck pain and improving spinal function.</strong></p>\n<h3>Advanced Back &amp; Neck Pain Center</h3>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/search/54+Omega+Drive,+Suite+F+Newark,+DE+19713?entry=gmail&amp;source=g\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">54 Omega Drive, Suite F</a><br/><a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/search/54+Omega+Drive,+Suite+F+Newark,+DE+19713?entry=gmail&amp;source=g\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Newark, DE 19713</a></p>",
