  "slug": "can-posture-be-connected-to-childhood-bedwetting-new-research-offers-an-interesting-clue",
  "title": "Can Posture Be Connected to Childhood Bedwetting? New Research Offers an Interesting Clue",
  "date": "2026-09-28",
  "excerpt": "When parents think about childhood bedwetting or urinary accidents, they usually think about the bladder, urinary tract, or developmental factors. But new research…",
  "html": "<p>When parents think about childhood bedwetting or urinary accidents, they usually think about the bladder, urinary tract, or developmental factors. But <strong>new research suggests that posture and musculoskeletal alignment may also be worth considering</strong>.</p>\n<p>A 2026 study published in <em>Scientific Reports</em> examined children with urinary incontinence and found measurable differences in <strong>postural alignment, particularly around the hips and pelvis</strong>. While the research does not prove that poor posture causes urinary problems, it raises an interesting question about the relationship between the musculoskeletal system and bladder and bowel function.</p>\n<h2>What Does Posture Have to Do With Urinary Function?</h2>\n<p>Our bodies work as interconnected systems. The spine, pelvis, hips, abdominal muscles, and pelvic floor all contribute to stability, movement, and normal function.</p>\n<p>In the study, researchers evaluated 53 children between the ages of 5 and 14, including children with daytime urinary incontinence and nighttime bedwetting. Children with urinary symptoms demonstrated differences in whole-body alignment, with some of the most notable differences occurring in the <strong>hip and pelvic region</strong>.</p>\n<p>The researchers also examined core-muscle function and its relationship to posture. Some relationships were identified in the initial analysis, although they did not remain statistically significant after additional statistical correction.</p>\n<p>That means more research is needed—but the findings are still interesting for parents and healthcare providers.</p>\n<h2>How Could Chiropractic Care Help?</h2>\n<p>At <strong>Advanced Back &amp; Neck Pain Center in Newark, Delaware</strong>, chiropractic care focuses on more than simply treating back or neck pain.</p>\n<p>A chiropractic evaluation can examine:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Spinal and pelvic alignment</li>\n<li>Hip mobility and symmetry</li>\n<li>Posture and movement patterns</li>\n<li>Core and postural muscle function</li>\n<li>Musculoskeletal restrictions and imbalances</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When appropriate, <strong>chiropractic care and physical therapy</strong> can be used together to improve mobility, strength, coordination, and overall musculoskeletal function.</p>\n<p>Importantly, chiropractic care should not be considered a replacement for appropriate medical evaluation of childhood urinary incontinence. Persistent bedwetting or urinary accidents can have many causes, and children should be evaluated by their pediatrician and, when necessary, a pediatric urologist or other qualified healthcare professional.</p>\n<h2>A New Reason to Look at the Whole Body</h2>\n<p>The most important takeaway from this research isn’t that chiropractic care is a treatment for bedwetting. The study doesn’t demonstrate that.</p>\n<p>Instead, it gives us another reason to consider the <strong>relationship between posture, the pelvis, core musculature, and normal body function</strong>.</p>\n<p>If your child is experiencing urinary or bowel symptoms along with concerns about posture, hip mobility, pelvic alignment, or musculoskeletal function, a chiropractic evaluation may be one piece of a comprehensive approach.</p>\n<h3>Chiropractic Care in Newark, Delaware</h3>\n<p>At <strong>Advanced Back &amp; Neck Pain Center</strong>, our approach combines <strong>chiropractic care, physical therapy, and massage therapy</strong> to evaluate and treat musculoskeletal problems from multiple perspectives.</p>\n<p>If you’re looking for a <strong>chiropractor in Newark, DE</strong>, our team can evaluate your child’s posture, movement, spine, hips, and overall musculoskeletal function and help determine whether conservative care may be appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Advanced Back &amp; Neck Pain Center</strong><br/><a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/search/54+Omega+Drive,+Suite+F+Newark,+DE+19713?entry=gmail&amp;source=g\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">54 Omega Drive, Suite F</a><br/><a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/search/54+Omega+Drive,+Suite+F+Newark,+DE+19713?entry=gmail&amp;source=g\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Newark, DE 19713</a></p>",
